        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b9ed69b6-e66a-5f2b-9d72-0ed9cb0738fb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hart Crane, John le Carré, T. S. Eliot, David John Moore Cornwell\n- Works: The Bridge, The Broken Tower, The Waste Land, The Russia House\n- Roles: British author, espionage novelist\n- Organizations: Security Service, Secret Intelligence Service\n- Concepts: modern urban culture, optimistic view\n\nFacts:\n- Hart Crane sought in his ambitious work \"The Bridge\" to write an epic poem in the vein of T. S. Eliot's \"The Waste Land\" that expressed a more optimistic view of modern urban culture than Eliot's work.\n- In 1932, Hart Crane's poem \"The Broken Tower\" was the last new poem intended to be published in his lifetime.\n- \"The Broken Tower\" has been widely regarded as one of the best lyrics of Hart Crane's final years, and possibly of his career.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was born on 19 October 1931 and became known by the pseudonym John le Carré.\n- During the 1950s and 1960s, John le Carré worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service and began writing novels under his pen name.\n- John le Carré is a British author of espionage novels.\n- In 1989, John le Carré published the spy novel \"The Russia House\".",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "fb4269ed-fb0e-5e32-b961-ace1328b47a4",
        "text": "Page: Hart Crane. Sentence: In his most ambitious work, \"The Bridge\", Crane sought to write an epic poem, in the vein of \"The Waste Land\", that expressed a more optimistic view of modern, urban culture than the one that he found in Eliot's work.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: David John Moore Cornwell (born 19 October 1931), known by the pseudonym John le Carré ( ), is a British author of espionage novels.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: During the 1950s and the 1960s, he worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service, and began writing novels under his pen name.\nPage: The Broken Tower. Sentence: The last new poem meant to be published in Hart Crane's life, \"The Broken Tower\" (1932) has been widely acknowledged as one of the best lyrics of Crane's last years, if not his career.\nPage: The Russia House. Sentence: The Russia House is a spy novel by John le Carré published in 1989.",
